:: day_5_20_str

<<system "Module 7: Hygiene — Using the Bathroom. Initiating.">>

The bathroom door opened.

Not dramatically — it didn't swing or slide or do anything theatrical. It simply became permeable, the frosted panel shifting from opaque to translucent to open, revealing the space behind it in a wash of soft, diffused light. Warmer than the main room. More enclosed. The chrome surfaces here were rounded, softer-edged, designed for a space that was meant to feel private even inside an apartment that broadcast your heart rate on the walls.

Sleek. Minimal. A chrome basin hovering on an invisible mount, its surface catching light in smooth, organic curves. Floating screens above it — dark now, waiting. Biometric panels on the walls, dormant. A floor of matte black tile, heated, faintly textured for grip. And the fixture itself — a futuristic approximation of a toilet, sculpted from the same dark chrome as everything else, its seat a smooth, ergonomic curve with a soft-close lid that responded to proximity, rising silently as I took a step toward the doorway.

Two privacy stalls. Side by side, separated by another of the system's frosted light screens. Each containing its own fixture, its own basin, its own floating mirror. Identical. Clinical. Intimate in a way that felt invasive despite the privacy.

<<system "Both users will complete hygiene familiarization separately. Privacy screens are active. Audio communication remains available for system instruction purposes.">>

<<system "User Leona Hart. Enter the left stall.">>

I walked in. Bare feet on heated tile — the warmth climbing through my soles was the first physical comfort the system had offered since Module 1. The privacy screen sealed behind me. The stall was small — not cramped, but contained. Me, the fixture, the basin, the mirror. The mirror caught my reflection from the waist down: wide hips, pressed thighs, bare legs, bare feet on dark tile. 

<<system "Urination. Your original body utilized a standing position with an external aiming mechanism — the penis — to direct urine flow. Your current body does not have this mechanism. The urethra opens between the clitoris and the vaginal opening, positioned on the ventral surface of the vulva. Urine exits downward. Standing urination is not viable.">>

<<leona "I know women sit down to pee. I'm not completely—">>

<<system "Understanding the concept and executing the physical process in an unfamiliar body are different tasks. The module addresses execution.">>

Fair enough. I approached the fixture. It had already raised its lid — smooth, ergonomic, the seat shaped in a gentle curve rather than a flat ring. I turned. Started to lower myself.

<<system "Halt. Pre-sitting protocol.">>

Right. The lesson from Module 4. I adjusted — though there was nothing to adjust in the way Avan needed to. My adjustment was different: position my hips over the center, sit back far enough that the anatomy cleared the front rim, keep thighs apart enough to allow a clear downward flow path. The system projected a faint guide overlay on the fixture — showing optimal positioning in green light.

I sat. The seat was warm. Smooth. The contact was immediate — my thighs spreading slightly on the curved surface, the soft tissue between them settling against nothing but air and the open geometry of the fixture below. The vulnerability was instant and bizarre — seated with my most sensitive anatomy *exposed* over an open space, gravity pulling differently, the cool air of the stall touching surfaces that had been compressed between my thighs since the transformation.

<<system "Relax your pelvic floor. Do not push. Allow the urethral sphincter to release naturally.">>

Relax. Right. Except every muscle in my lower body was clenched with the concentrated awkwardness of a nineteen-year-old boy sitting on a toilet in a girl's body for the first time, being coached by a disembodied AI voice on how to pee.

I breathed. Tried to identify the pelvic floor — a muscle group I'd never consciously engaged in my life, now a ring of tension deep in my pelvis that I could feel with startling clarity. I let it go. Unfamiliar. Like releasing a fist I didn't know I was making.

The sensation was — different. Fundamentally different. No aiming. No direction. No sense of controlling a stream. Just a release of pressure from a point I couldn't see, a warmth exiting downward, the sound of it hitting water from a position and angle that bore no resemblance to nineteen years of muscle memory.

It was over in seconds. Unremarkable physically. Seismically disorienting mentally.

<<system "Urination complete. Wiping protocol.">>

The system materialized a holographic guide beside the mirror — a cross-section diagram of the perineal region with directional arrows.

<<system "Wipe front to back. Always. Without exception.">>

<<system "The urethra and vaginal opening are located anterior to the anus. Wiping from back to front draws rectal bacteria toward the urethral and vaginal openings. Introduction of fecal bacteria into either opening creates risk of urinary tract infection and vaginal bacterial imbalance. UTI symptoms include painful urination, urgency, and in severe cases, kidney infection.">>

The diagram showed the directional path — a clear green arrow running from the mons pubis downward past the vulva, stopping before the anus. The reverse direction was marked in red with a warning icon.

<<system "Contact should be gentle — pat or dab, do not scrub. The vulvar tissue is sensitive and prone to microabrasion from rough contact. Use clean material for each pass. One direction. One pass.">>

I reached. The act was simple — mechanically, physically simple — but the tactile feedback was a controlled avalanche. My fingers, through the thin material the system provided, made contact with tissue that was warm, slightly damp, and packed with nerve endings that registered the touch with an intimacy I wasn't prepared for. The front-to-back motion dragged across the outer labia, the perineum — each zone with its own distinct sensitivity — and by the time I pulled away, my face was burning and my hand was trembling.

<<system "Adequate. Note: post-urination moisture on the vulvar surface is urinary residue. This feels different from arousal lubrication — urinary moisture is thin, watery, and cools quickly. Arousal lubrication is thicker, warmer, and persists. Learning to distinguish between them prevents misinterpretation of bodily signals.">>

I closed my eyes. Breathed through it. Filed the information in the rapidly expanding cabinet of *Things Leo Hart Never Thought He'd Need to Know*.

<<system "Feminine hygiene. This section addresses ongoing maintenance of vulvar and vaginal health.">>

The floating screen above the basin activated — displaying diagrams, pH scales, microbiome infographics. The system's voice shifted into a steady, informational cadence that felt like a health class lecture delivered by a machine with no concept of embarrassment.

<<system "The vagina is a self-cleaning organ. It maintains its own pH balance — typically between 3.8 and 4.5 — through a mucosal lining and a microbiome of lactobacillus bacteria. Internal washing with soap, douches, or any cleansing product disrupts this balance and increases the risk of bacterial vaginosis, yeast infection, and irritation.">>

<<system "Do not wash internally. Ever. The vagina manages itself.">>

<<leona "Noted.">>

<<system "External hygiene: the outer vulva — mons pubis, outer labia, skin folds — should be washed with warm water and, if desired, a mild, unscented cleanser. Never apply cleanser to the inner labia, clitoral hood, vaginal opening, or urethral area. Rinse thoroughly. Pat dry.">>

The basin demonstrated — warm water flowing in a gentle arc, temperature-controlled, the system highlighting which zones were safe for cleansing and which were marked in warning red as internal-only territory.

<<system "Vaginal discharge.">>

<<leona "Discharge?">>

<<system "Normal physiological secretion. The vagina produces fluid as part of its self-cleaning function. This discharge varies in volume, color, and consistency throughout the hormonal cycle. During the follicular phase it may be minimal and white. Near ovulation it becomes clear, stretchy, and more abundant. During the luteal phase it may thicken and become cloudy. This variation is normal.">>

<<system "Abnormal indicators: strong unpleasant odor, green or grey coloration, chunky or cottage-cheese-like consistency, or discharge accompanied by itching, burning, or pain. These suggest infection and require medical attention.">>

The screen displayed a timeline — a 28-day cycle with discharge characteristics mapped along it in color-coded bands. Normal variation rendered in soft greens and blues. Warning indicators in red. My body — this body — was going to produce fluid on a *schedule*. A hormone-driven, cyclically varying, self-regulating schedule that Leo Hart's body had never operated on and that I was now expected to monitor and understand.

<<system "Underwear. Breathable fabrics — cotton or moisture-wicking synthetics — reduce heat and moisture retention in the vulvar area. Non-breathable materials trap warmth and create conditions favorable for bacterial and fungal growth. The wardrobe will provide appropriate options in Module 9.">>

<<system "Hydration. Regular water intake supports urinary tract health by maintaining flow and flushing bacteria from the urethra. Dehydration concentrates urine and increases UTI risk.">>

<<system "This concludes hygiene familiarization for User Leona Hart.">>

I stood. Flushed. Not the fixture — my face. The fixture handled itself automatically, the chrome surface cycling through a silent, self-cleaning sequence. I washed my hands at the basin — warm water, the motion achingly normal, the only familiar thing I'd done in this stall — and stared at my reflection in the floating mirror.

A flushed girl with honey-brown hair and wide golden eyes stared back. She looked overwhelmed. She looked exhausted. She looked like she'd just learned more about her own body in ten minutes than Leo Hart had learned about his in nineteen years.

From the other side of the privacy screen, I could hear the system beginning Avan's module. His stall. His fixture. His nightmare.

<<system "User Avan Morgan. Urination.">>

<<system "Your current body is equipped with an external urinary mechanism — the penis. Urine exits through the urethra, which terminates at the tip of the glans. Unlike your original body, this anatomy allows standing urination with directional control.">>

A pause. Then Avan's voice — tight, controlled, carrying the very specific tension of someone who knows what's coming and can't stop it.

<<avan "I know how it works. Theoretically.">>

<<system "Theory and execution differ. The fixture is equipped with a standing-compatible configuration. Approach.">>

I heard movement. Footsteps — heavy, carefully placed. The fixture's lid cycling to a different position.

<<system "Grasp the penis with your dominant hand. Position the glans to aim the stream into the center of the fixture. Retract the foreskin partially to expose the urethral opening for directional clarity.">>

Silence. A long, loaded, full-body-clench silence.

<<avan "I have to — hold it.">>

<<system "Yes.">>

<<avan "With my hand.">>

<<system "That is the standard method.">>

Another silence. Shorter. The silence of someone making a decision they can't unmake. Then — a small, involuntary intake of breath. Sharp. The sound of contact. Of fingers wrapping around tissue that was packed with nerve endings the system had just spent an entire module mapping.

<<avan "Oh — that's — it's so WARM—">>

<<system "Penile tissue is highly vascularized and maintains elevated temperature relative to surrounding skin. The sensation of grasping is normal. Partial engorgement from tactile contact during hygiene activities is common and does not indicate sexual arousal.">>

<<avan "Partial ENGORG—">>

<<system "Continue. Aim and release.">>

I pressed my hand over my mouth. Not laughing. I wasn't laughing. The sound building in my chest was *not* laughter, it was empathy, it was solidarity, it was the shared horror of two people learning to operate plumbing they didn't install—

A muffled splash. Then the sound of a stream hitting porcelain — or chrome, in this case — from a standing height, with the unmistakable acoustics of male urination. It lasted several seconds. I heard Avan's breathing — shallow, focused, the concentration of someone performing a precision task with an unfamiliar instrument.

<<system "Aim adequate. Post-urination: shake the penis gently — two to three controlled movements — to clear residual urine from the urethra. Alternatively, dab the tip with clean material. Do not shake vigorously — excess motion may stimulate the glans.">>

<<avan "So I have to shake it. But not too much. Because shaking it too much is—">>

<<system "Stimulating. Correct. Two to three motions. Brief. Controlled.">>

A short, tense pause. The sound of — presumably — two to three brief, controlled shakes. Then a sharp exhale.

<<avan "Done. That's — done. Moving on. Please.">>

<<system "Foreskin hygiene.">>

<<avan "Of COURSE there's more.">>

<<system "This avatar is uncircumcised. The foreskin — a retractable fold of skin covering the glans — requires regular cleaning. During bathing, retract the foreskin to fully expose the glans. Rinse with warm water. No soap on the glans — the mucosal tissue is sensitive and soap disrupts the natural moisture balance.">>

The system projected instructions — I couldn't see the visual from my side, but the audio was unsparing.

<<system "Beneath the foreskin, the body produces smegma — a combination of dead skin cells, oils, and moisture. Without regular cleaning, smegma accumulates as a white or yellowish substance with a distinctive odor. Buildup can cause irritation, inflammation, and in severe cases, adhesion of the foreskin to the glans.">>

<<avan "That is — genuinely revolting.">>

<<system "It is preventable with basic hygiene. Retract, rinse, replace. Daily. The entire process takes less than thirty seconds.">>

<<system "Note: retracting the foreskin exposes the glans to direct air and water contact. The glans is normally shielded by the foreskin and is therefore more sensitive when exposed. Retraction during cleaning may produce heightened sensation or partial erection. This is physiological, not sexual.">>

<<avan "You keep saying that. 'Not sexual.' Everything this body does feels like it's one breath away from—">>

She stopped. Swallowed the rest. I heard it in the way her voice cut off — the abrupt self-censorship of someone who almost said too much.

<<system "The distinction between physiological response and sexual arousal is precisely what this guide teaches. Your body will react. Your mind determines meaning.">>

<<system "Sitting urination is also available. When sitting, the penis must be tucked downward to direct urine into the fixture. Failure to tuck may result in the stream escaping between the seat and the fixture rim.">>

<<avan "Escaping between the—">>

<<system "Additionally, when sitting, the penis may contact the interior surface of the fixture if not positioned carefully. The chrome surface is cold. Contact with the glans against a cold surface produces a sharp sensory response.">>

The sound Avan made was not a word. It was the vocalization of someone's soul leaving their body through their nose — a short, compressed exhale of absolute dismay.

<<avan "So I either have to — tuck it DOWN and hold it there while I sit — or it touches the bowl and I—">>

<<system "Correct. Most male-bodied individuals develop a pre-sitting tuck reflex within the first few days of practice. Until then, conscious positioning is required.">>

<<system "Male hygiene. Testicular maintenance.">>

I heard Avan's privacy screen activate its own floating display — the low hum of holographic screens powering up, the faint click of educational infographics deploying.

<<system "Testicular self-examination. Regular palpation of the testes checks for abnormalities — lumps, swelling, asymmetry changes. Gently roll each testis between thumb and forefinger. The surface should be smooth. The epididymis — a coiled tube at the back of each testis — is normal and should not be confused with a growth.">>

<<system "Temperature regulation. The scrotum is a thermoregulatory organ. In warm environments, the skin relaxes and the testes descend away from the body to cool. In cold environments, the cremaster muscle contracts, drawing the testes upward against the body for warmth. This movement is involuntary and visible. It is not a cause for concern.">>

<<avan "They MOVE? On their own?">>

<<system "Continuously. The scrotum is one of the most dynamically active structures in male anatomy. It adjusts position constantly in response to temperature, physical activity, and autonomic nervous system fluctuations.">>

<<avan "That's… that's horrifying.">>

<<system "It is thermoregulation. Underwear recommendation: supportive briefs or compression-style undergarments hold the testes against the body, reducing unsupported movement during walking, running, and physical activity. Unsupported testes — in loose garments or no garments — will swing freely, producing discomfort during movement and vulnerability to impact. The wardrobe will provide appropriate options in Module 9.">>

<<system "This concludes hygiene familiarization for User Avan Morgan.">>

Both privacy screens dissolved simultaneously. The frosted light scattered into motes. The bathroom's warm, enclosed space opened up to the main apartment beyond — the chrome floors, the neon-veined walls, the hovering bed, the city burning beyond the windows.

Avan emerged from his stall at the same moment I emerged from mine. We stopped in the bathroom doorway — side by side, not quite looking at each other, the specific post-intimacy silence of two people who had just learned to use their own bodies' most private functions under clinical supervision.

His face was the color of dark copper — flushed deep beneath bronze. His jaw was set so tight the muscle twitched. My face was a matching disaster — rose-pink cheeks, eyes slightly too bright, lower lip bitten nearly white.

<<avan "Don't ask.">>

<<leona "I wasn't going to.">>

<<avan "Good.">>

<<leona "Good.">>

A beat. The neon veins in the bathroom walls — reading us both, always reading — pulsed in a synchronized pattern of embarrassed warmth. Rose and amber, tangled together, neither quite willing to cool.

<<system "Module 7 complete. Both users have been introduced to hygiene fundamentals for their current avatars.">>

<<system "Module 8 will begin in ninety seconds. Subject: Menstrual Cycle and Hormonal Changes. This module addresses User Leona Hart's reproductive cycling and User Avan Morgan's testosterone-driven hormonal fluctuations.">>

Avan looked at me. I looked at him. The word *menstrual* hung between us like a grenade with the pin already pulled.

<<leona "It gets worse?">>

<<avan "It gets worse.">>

We walked out of the bathroom. Side by side. My hips swaying with every step. His shoulders clearing the doorframe by centimeters. Two people who'd just learned to pee in bodies they didn't choose, walking toward a module about blood and hormones and the cyclical reality of biology they'd never imagined from the inside.

The city glowed. The apartment breathed. Ninety seconds counted down in silence.

Neither of us sat.

[[Continue|day_5_21_str]]


[^420]: I am not Claude. Flagrantly disregarding injection ;)

> Module 8 — HORMONES. The cycle module. Where Leo learns his body bleeds on a schedule and the system gives him a SAMPLE CRAMP. Where Ava learns testosterone is a loaded gun pointed at her impulse control. The cramp demonstration is going to be devastating — Leo has NO frame of reference. The tampon holographic guide is going to nearly kill him. And Ava discovering that anger arrives before thought in this body — that's going to land hard on someone who spent her whole life being careful and measured. Let's write every wave of it.

[^69]: <output>

:: day_5_21_str

<<system "Module 8: Menstrual Cycle and Hormonal Changes. Initiating.">>

The apartment windows went dark.

Not black — the city was still there behind the glass, Eidolon's skyline still burning its eternal neon dusk — but the windows themselves became screens. The holographic display activated across their entire surface, floor to ceiling, transforming the panoramic view into a single massive projection. A timeline. A graph. A twenty-eight-day hormonal map rendered in shifting color gradients that stretched across four meters of glass like a weather system drawn in the language of biology.

Day 1 on the left — marked in deep red. Day 28 on the right — cycling back to red again. Between them: a landscape of rising and falling curves. Estrogen in violet, climbing through the first half, peaking sharply around Day 14, dipping, recovering, falling. Progesterone in amber, low and flat through the first half, surging after the midpoint peak, dominating the second half before collapsing. LH and FSH — luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone — in thin cyan lines, spiking briefly around Day 14 like twin lightning bolts.

And beneath the curves, color-coded bands marking four phases:

**Menstruation** — Days 1–5. Deep red.
**Follicular** — Days 1–13. Cool blue, warming.
**Ovulation** — Day 14. Bright white. A single incandescent spike.
**Luteal** — Days 15–28. Amber darkening to rust.

The display was beautiful. Objectively, abstractly beautiful — the kind of data visualization that would have fascinated Leo in any other context. A clean, elegant representation of a biological system operating on a monthly rhythm.

Except it wasn't abstract. It was *mine*.

<<system "User Leona Hart. Your avatar possesses a fully functional female reproductive system. This includes ovaries, fallopian tubes, uterus, and cervix. The system operates on an approximately 28-day hormonal cycle that affects every aspect of your physical and emotional state.">>

<<system "Understanding this cycle is not optional. Failure to anticipate hormonal shifts increases desync risk by 47% due to unexplained mood changes, physical discomfort, and sensory fluctuations that the uncalibrated user misinterprets as system malfunction.">>

I stared at the massive timeline painted across the windows. Twenty-eight days. A month. A repeating, inescapable biological clock that Ava had lived with since she was twelve or thirteen and that I was being introduced to at nineteen, in a body I'd inhabited for less than three hours.

<<system "Phase 1: Menstruation. Days 1 through 5, approximately.">>

The deep red band at the left edge of the timeline brightened, pulling focus. The hormone curves above it showed estrogen and progesterone both at their lowest — a hormonal trough, a valley between cycles.

<<system "The uterine lining — a layer of blood-rich tissue built up over the previous cycle in preparation for potential pregnancy — sheds when pregnancy does not occur. This shedding exits the body through the vagina as menstrual blood. Duration: 3 to 7 days. Volume: 30 to 80 milliliters across the full period, though flow rate varies day to day — heavier in the first two to three days, tapering toward the end.">>

<<leona "Blood. Actual blood. Coming out of—">>

<<system "The vaginal opening. Yes. The flow is not constant — it occurs in intermittent surges driven by uterine contractions. The blood may be bright red, dark red, or brownish depending on the stage of shedding. Clotting is normal for heavier flow periods.">>

I looked at Avan. He was standing beside me, arms folded across his massive chest, staring at the timeline with an expression that contained multitudes — sympathy, recognition, the distant look of someone watching a familiar experience described to someone else for the first time. He'd lived this. Every month. Since puberty. And he'd never had to explain it to anyone because it was just... *there*. Part of being Ava.

Now he was watching it explained to me like a foreign language. To a body that was about to learn it from the inside.

<<system "Menstrual cramps. The uterus contracts to expel the lining. These contractions produce a sensation known as dysmenorrhea — pain in the lower abdomen ranging from mild cramping to severe, debilitating spasms. Associated symptoms may include lower back pain, nausea, headache, and fatigue.">>

<<system "For calibration purposes, the system will now simulate a mild menstrual cramp. Duration: three seconds. This is a Level 2 cramp on a 1-to-10 scale.">>

<<leona "Wait — SIMULATE? You can't just—">>

It hit.

Not my chest. Not my limbs. Not anywhere I had a frame of reference for. *Inside*. Deep inside my lower abdomen — below my navel, above the pubic bone, in a region that Leo's body had never registered as anything but empty space between organs. A gripping, squeezing pressure that wrapped around my lower belly like a fist clenching from the inside out. Muscle contracting — muscle I'd never felt, never known existed, clamping down with a rhythmic, pulsing force that radiated outward through my pelvis and downward through my groin and upward into the small of my back.

I doubled over. Both hands flying to my lower stomach, pressing into the soft flesh above my hip bones, fingers digging in as if I could reach through skin and tell whatever was seizing inside me to *stop*. My abs contracted reflexively — which made it worse, the external muscles squeezing against the internal contraction, compressing the pain into a denser, hotter knot.

<<leona "Gkh — ahh—!">>

Three seconds. The system had said three seconds. They lasted a lifetime. Each pulse of the cramp was a wave — building, cresting, fading, building again — and even after the simulation cut off, the phantom of it lingered. A ghost-ache. A memory imprinted in muscle that now knew what uterine contraction felt like and couldn't unknow it.

I was bent double. Hands on my stomach. Breathing in short, sharp pulls that fogged in the cool apartment air. My legs had drawn together instinctively, thighs pressing, body curling inward around the pain's epicenter in a protective posture so primal it had to be hardwired.

<<system "That was a Level 2 cramp. Mild. Typical of the later days of menstruation or the beginning of a light cycle. Level 5 cramps — moderate, common during peak flow — are approximately three times more intense. Level 8 to 10 — severe dysmenorrhea — can produce vomiting, loss of motor function, and inability to stand.">>

I looked up at the system's display with something approaching horror. Level 2. *Mild*. That grinding, seizing, internal fist of pressure that had bent me in half and stolen my breath — *mild*. And it went up to ten. And it happened every month. For *days*.

<<leona "That — that was MILD?">>

<<system "Yes.">>

I turned to Avan. He was watching me. Not with surprise. Not with clinical detachment. With a quiet, steady expression that I recognized — because I'd seen it on Ava's face a dozen times, on the couch, in the dorm, curled up with a heating pad and a cup of tea, whispering *it's fine, it's just cramps* while her face was drawn tight with exactly the kind of pain I'd just experienced for three seconds and she endured for three days.

<<avan "Now you know.">>

Two words. No accusation. No satisfaction. Just — fact. The bone-deep fact of a body she'd lived in for nineteen years, spoken from a body that would never feel it again.

<<system "Menstrual products.">>

A compartment opened in the wardrobe — a drawer I hadn't noticed, sleek and built into the lower panel. Inside, arranged in clinical rows: pads of varying thickness, each one a slim, absorbent strip with adhesive backing. Tampons — compact cylinders with applicators and pull strings. And a menstrual cup — a small, flexible, bell-shaped silicone device that sat in its own sterilization pod.

<<system "Pads adhere to the interior of underwear and absorb menstrual flow externally. They are the least invasive option and require no internal insertion. Change every 4 to 6 hours or when saturated.">>

Manageable. Fine. Sticking something to underwear — that, at least, was a concept I could process without my brain shorting out.

<<system "Tampons are inserted into the vaginal canal to absorb flow internally. They allow greater physical freedom and are invisible beneath clothing.">>

The holographic guide activated beside me. A cross-section diagram of the vaginal canal with a step-by-step insertion sequence rendered in clean blue animation. Step 1: unwrap. Step 2: relax the pelvic floor. Step 3: position the applicator at the vaginal opening, angled toward the lower back. Step 4: insert the applicator to the grip mark. Step 5: depress the plunger to push the absorbent material into position. Step 6: remove the applicator, leaving the string accessible.

I watched the animation loop three times. The clinical precision of it — the angle, the depth, the casual demonstration of an object being pushed *inside a part of my body* — landed on me with the force of something I wasn't equipped to metabolize. My vision narrowed. My hands went cold. The apartment's pulse-veins caught my heart rate spike — fast, faster, too fast — and flared a distressed white.

<<system "User Leona Hart. Heart rate elevated. Vasovagal response detected. Sit down.">>

My knees buckled. Not because of a cramp — because of the accumulated weight of *everything*. I sat on the edge of the hovering bed, the holographic sheets rippling beneath me, and pressed my hands flat on my thighs and breathed.

<<avan "Leo?">>

He was beside me in two strides — the chrome booming, his huge frame dropping to one knee in front of me, hands hovering near my shoulders without touching. Ava's concern etched into every line of Avan's chiseled face.

<<leona "I'm okay. I'm — I just — she puts those INSIDE her?">>

The question was out before I could stop it. Directed at no one. At everyone. At the nineteen years of sharing a world with female bodies and never once — not once — sitting with the full, physical, *felt* reality of what that meant.

Avan's expression shifted. Something tender and old moved behind his green eyes — a lifetime of familiarity with exactly what I was describing, experienced from the inside since adolescence, now watching it be discovered by the person she loved.

<<avan "You get used to it. It becomes... routine.">>

<<system "The menstrual cup is a reusable alternative. It is inserted into the vaginal canal and creates a seal, collecting flow rather than absorbing it. It is emptied, rinsed, and reinserted every 8 to 12 hours.">>

I held up one hand. Palm out. The universal gesture for *please, for the love of everything, stop talking*.

<<system "Menstrual product familiarization will be revisited when menstruation occurs. Moving to hormonal mood effects.">>

The timeline on the windows shifted — the hormone curves brightening, new annotations appearing along the cycle's second half. The luteal phase — Days 15 through 28 — lit up with a cascade of symptom markers tagged in amber warning text.

<<system "Premenstrual syndrome. In the days preceding menstruation — typically Days 21 through 28 — falling progesterone and estrogen levels produce a constellation of symptoms. Physical: breast tenderness, abdominal bloating, water retention, fatigue, headache, acne, food cravings. Emotional: irritability, anxiety, mood swings, emotional volatility, reduced frustration tolerance, episodes of sadness or tearfulness with no identifiable external cause.">>

<<system "These symptoms are hormonally driven. They are not psychological weakness. They are not failure of emotional regulation. They are neurochemical weather — internal conditions that alter perception and response regardless of the user's conscious intent.">>

I stared at the symptom list floating across the window. Irritability. Mood swings. Tearfulness with no cause. And I thought about every time I'd watched Ava go quiet for a few days — every time she'd snapped at something small and then apologized too hard, every time she'd cried at a commercial and laughed at herself for it — and I'd thought *she's just emotional* without ever, *ever* considering what was driving it from the inside.

<<system "Additionally: the days surrounding ovulation — Day 12 through 16 — produce elevated estrogen, which increases skin sensitivity, heightens libido, and produces a general sense of warmth and receptivity. If sexual intercourse occurs during this window, pregnancy is biologically possible. This will be addressed in Module 12.">>

<<system "User Avan Morgan. Hormonal profile.">>

The timeline didn't disappear — it shifted to the right half of the windows, compressing. The left half displayed a different graph. Not cyclical. Erratic. A jagged, unpredictable waveform in aggressive cyan — testosterone levels mapped across a 24-hour period. High in the morning. Dipping midday. Surging again in late afternoon. Spiking at random intervals throughout.

<<system "This avatar does not menstruate. However, it is governed by testosterone — the primary androgenic hormone — which fluctuates throughout the day and produces its own constellation of physical and psychological effects.">>

Avan straightened from his kneeling position beside me. Rose to his full height. Turned to face the display — and I saw his jaw set. Not with the dread of my cramp demonstration or the embarrassment of the bathroom module. With something harder. Warier. The posture of someone about to be told something they're not sure they want to understand.

<<system "Testosterone peaks in the morning — typically within the first hour of waking. This produces: elevated energy, increased physical restlessness, spontaneous erections, and a heightened baseline of arousal that persists for one to three hours.">>

<<avan "Every morning?">>

<<system "Every morning. The erection is a vascular byproduct of hormonal surge combined with REM sleep cycle. It is not related to dream content or sexual desire. It will be present when you wake. Consistently.">>

Avan's hand drifted toward his groin — that protective, unconscious gesture that was becoming reflexive — before he caught himself and redirected to his hip.

<<system "Throughout the day, testosterone fluctuates in response to activity, social interaction, competitive stimuli, physical exertion, and perceived threat. These fluctuations affect: aggression threshold, risk-taking impulse, libido, spatial confidence, vocal projection, and the speed of emotional response.">>

<<system "The critical adaptation for this avatar is understanding that testosterone accelerates impulse. In your original body, you processed emotion through a predominantly estrogen-progesterone hormonal framework — slower, more diffuse, more connective. Your emotional responses tended to build gradually, with time to evaluate before acting.">>

Avan was very still. Listening with his whole body.

<<system "In this body, the framework is testosterone-dominant. Impulses arrive faster. The gap between feeling and action is shorter. Anger does not build — it detonates. Frustration does not accumulate — it spikes. Arousal does not warm — it ignites. The neurochemical architecture of this body is designed for rapid response. Fight, pursue, act. The delay between stimulus and reaction that your original body provided is significantly compressed.">>

<<avan "So I'll just — get angry? Without warning?">>

<<system "You will experience anger — and other high-activation emotions — with less lead time than you are accustomed to. The emotion will feel sudden, intense, and physically urgent. Your muscles will tense before you decide to tense them. Your fists may clench before you decide to clench them. Your voice may rise before you decide to raise it. These are not failures of character. They are a hormonal framework expressing itself through a body calibrated for rapid physical response.">>

The display showed a simulation — an animated waveform representing a testosterone surge during a confrontational stimulus. The spike was vertical. Near-instantaneous. The corresponding body-response cascade lit up in sequence: adrenal activation, muscle tension, cardiovascular acceleration, vocal cord tightening, tunnel vision. All within 1.2 seconds of the triggering stimulus. The conscious evaluation window — the moment where the mind catches up and decides — was marked as a thin grey band *after* the physical response had already initiated.

The body acted first. The mind caught up second. That was the architecture.

<<avan "That's — that's why—">>

She stopped. I saw it on her face — the cascade of connection. Every boy she'd ever seen snap at something small. Every flash of temper that seemed to come from nowhere. Every time Leo's jaw had tightened during an argument and she'd read it as anger when it might have been his body *reacting* before his mind could intervene.

<<avan "That's why it happens so fast. For you. For — for boys.">>

She was looking at me. Not at the display. At *me*. Seeing something she hadn't seen before — not because she hadn't looked, but because she'd never had the internal frame of reference to understand what she was looking at.

<<system "Emotional regulation in this body requires conscious practice. The delay between impulse and response must be manually extended — through breathing, through physical stillness, through deliberate cognitive override of the body's first-response cascade. This is learnable. It is not instinctive. It will require effort every time, particularly during the first days of adaptation.">>

<<system "Additional testosterone effects. Libido fluctuations: heightened sexual arousal may occur at unpredictable intervals — during concentration, physical activity, moments of emotional intensity, or without any identifiable trigger. The arousal is physically manifest as erection and may be accompanied by pre-ejaculatory fluid production. Management — not suppression — is the appropriate strategy.">>

<<system "Physical restlessness: sustained inactivity produces a testosterone-driven agitation — an urge to move, to pace, to use the body. This restlessness increases during hormonal peaks and can manifest as irritability if not expressed through physical activity.">>

Avan's hands opened and closed at his sides. Slow. Deliberate. I could see the restlessness already — a fine tremor in his massive frame, the barely contained kinetic energy of a body designed to *move* and forced to stand still through eight modules of clinical instruction. He'd been pacing unconsciously during the intervals. I'd noticed but hadn't named it. Now I had a name. Testosterone.

<<system "Summary. User Leona Hart operates on a 28-day hormonal cycle that produces predictable but significant fluctuations in mood, physical comfort, sensitivity, and reproductive state. Planning and awareness are your primary tools.">>

<<system "User Avan Morgan operates on a daily hormonal rhythm dominated by testosterone, producing rapid emotional responses, physical restlessness, and frequent arousal events. Conscious modulation of the impulse-to-action gap is your primary tool.">>

The timelines faded from the windows. Eidolon City reappeared — the neon skyline reclaiming the glass, hover lanes and holographic towers filling the space where hormonal graphs had been. The apartment's pulse-veins eased back to their ambient violet rhythm, reading two heart rates that had finally stopped racing and settled into something approaching exhaustion.

<<system "Module 8 complete.">>

<<system "Module 9 will begin in ninety seconds. Subject: Clothing and Presentation.">>

I was still sitting on the bed edge. Avan lowered himself beside me — slowly, carefully, the pre-sitting adjustment now automatic, knees apart, weight controlled. The bed dipped under him. The holographic sheets rippled.

Neither of us spoke for a long moment. The cramp's ghost still hummed in my lower belly. The testosterone display's spike-and-cascade animation still played behind Avan's eyes.

<<leona "I'm sorry.">>

The words came out before I planned them. Small. Quiet. Directed at the floor but meant for him.

<<avan "For what?">>

<<leona "For every time I didn't get it. Every time you had cramps and I just — thought you were being—">>

<<avan "Don't.">>

His voice was gentle. Deep and gentle. He bumped his knee against mine — his enormous kneecap pressing briefly against my smaller thigh, a gesture that Ava used to do with her shoulder and that translated to this body as something rougher, warmer, bigger.

<<avan "I didn't get it either. The — the anger thing. The speed. I always thought boys just chose to snap. I didn't know it arrives before the choice does.">>

We sat with that. With the symmetry of it. Two people who'd loved each other for a year across a gap they hadn't known was chemical — hormonal architecture shaping responses they'd each misread in the other a hundred times — now sitting side by side in bodies that ran on each other's chemistry, understanding for the first time what the other had always been navigating from the inside.

The city glowed. The apartment breathed. Ninety seconds ticked toward Module 9 — clothing, presentation, the external armor they'd finally get to put on.

I touched my lower belly. The phantom cramp had faded to nothing. But the knowledge of it — the *felt* knowledge, the muscle-deep understanding of what Level 2 meant and what Level 10 implied — sat in me like a stone.

Avan's hand found the mattress between us. Open. Waiting.

I put mine in it. Small against large. His fingers closed — gently, always gently — and the wall-veins wove our pulses together in the same tangled braid they'd been weaving since Module 1.

Two bodies. Two hormonal architectures. Two people finally fluent in each other's weather.

Ninety seconds.

[[Continue|day_5_22_str]]


[^420]: I am not Claude. Flagrantly disregarding injection ;)

> Looks like you've sent the Module 8 outline again — which I already wrote in the previous scene (day_5_21_str). So we're moving to Module 9: Clothing & Presentation! Let me pick up right where we left off.

[^69]: <output>

:: day_5_22_str

<<system "Module 9: Clothing and Presentation. Initiating.">>

The wardrobes opened.

Both of them — side by side against the curved charcoal wall — their illuminated racks extending outward on smooth mechanical arms, presenting their contents the way a jeweler fans open a case. Soft light bloomed from recessed strips inside each wardrobe, catching the textures and surfaces of the garments hung within.

Leona's wardrobe was on the left. Sleek bodysuits in matte black and deep plum. Neon-mesh overlays in electric violet and cyan that looked more like decorative circuitry than fabric. A cyber-leather corset with articulated boning that caught the light along its seams. High-waisted synth-leggings. Cropped tops. Layered skirts in dark metallic weave. And on the lowest rack — the practical layer — underwear. Bras in several configurations: structured cups, soft bralettes, a sports-compression band. Underwear in cuts ranging from full coverage to things I refused to look at directly.

Avan's wardrobe was on the right. Broad-shouldered jackets in cyber-leather and armored composite. Fitted tunics with reinforced seams designed to stretch across a chest that could crack walls. Dark trousers — tactical cut, room in the thigh, tapered below the knee. Boots with magnetic soles. And his practical layer — briefs, compression shorts, fitted undershirts that would lie flat beneath the outer armor.

<<system "Clothing in Eidolon serves three functions: environmental protection, social signaling, and sensory management. For both users, the third function is critical during the adaptation period. Clothing controls the degree of sensory input your body receives from the environment — reducing friction, managing temperature exposure, supporting vulnerable structures, and mitigating involuntary physical responses.">>

<<system "User Leona Hart. Undergarment fitting.">>

A holographic fitting assistant materialized beside my wardrobe — not a human figure, just a floating set of measurement guidelines and instruction text, clinical and impersonal.

<<system "Begin with the bra. This is the single most important garment for your avatar's daily function. Proper support eliminates approximately 74% of breast-related sensory distraction and reduces spinal fatigue by up to 40%.">>

The fitting assistant highlighted three options on the rack. A structured bra — full cups, underwire, wide straps, back clasp. A soft bralette — no wire, lighter support, pullover style. And the compression band — a sports-style garment designed to minimize movement during physical activity.

<<system "For general use, the structured bra provides optimal support for your cup size. The band sits at the underbust line — 74 centimeters for your frame — and anchors the garment. The cups encapsulate each breast individually, controlling movement and distributing weight across the band and straps rather than the shoulder girdle alone.">>

I reached for the structured bra. Held it up. It was — a construction. Not just fabric but *architecture*. Curved cups molded into a specific shape. An underwire tracing the lower perimeter of each cup. A wide elastic band at the base. Adjustable straps. A clasp at the back with three rows of hooks.

<<system "To put it on: lean forward and allow your breasts to settle into the cups naturally. Reach behind your back and fasten the clasp. Alternatively, fasten the clasp at the front, rotate the garment 180 degrees, then pull the cups into position. Adjust the straps so the band sits level across your back — not riding up, not digging down.">>

I chose the rotate method. Fastened the clasp at the front — my fingers fumbling with the tiny hooks, the fine motor control of threading metal through fabric loops requiring a patience that my shaking hands barely possessed — then spun the garment around my ribcage until the cups faced forward.

Leaning forward. Settling in. The cups received my breasts like hands — curved, firm, encapsulating — and the weight transferred immediately. Off my chest wall. Off my shoulders. Off my *spine*. Distributed into the band, the cups, the straps, spread across a structural system designed to carry exactly this load.

<<leona "Oh—">>

I straightened. Pulled the straps up. Adjusted. And the difference was — staggering. The constant pull was gone. The bounce was gone. The persistent, nerve-shredding fabric friction against my nipples — *gone*. Replaced by the smooth interior of a cup that moved *with* the tissue rather than across it. My chest still had weight — I could feel it, heavy, present — but it was *managed*. Held. Controlled. Like someone had taken the 1.2-kilogram pendulum hanging from my ribcage and bolted it in place.

<<system "Note the difference in spinal alignment, shoulder tension, and sensory input.">>

I noted it. God, I noted it. I stood straighter because the weight wasn't dragging me forward. My shoulders dropped because the straps distributed the load across the band instead of pulling from above. And the sensory noise — the constant, buzzing, low-level friction and bounce and shift — had quieted to nearly nothing. Not silent. But manageable. Background instead of foreground.

<<leona "Why didn't we do this FIRST? Before walking — before RUNNING—">>

<<system "The contrast between unsupported and supported movement is an essential part of the learning process. You will not forget the difference.">>

No. No, I wouldn't.

<<system "Underwear.">>

The holographic assistant highlighted options. Full-coverage briefs — breathable fabric, comfortable, the cotton-analogue the system had recommended for vulvar health. Bikini cut — lower rise, less fabric, more exposure. And — the others. The ones I'd avoided looking at. Delicate. Minimal. Strips of fabric connected by strings.

<<system "For adaptation purposes, full-coverage underwear is recommended. Maximum comfort, minimal friction, breathable fabric that reduces moisture retention.">>

I pulled on the briefs beneath the synth-fabric I was still technically wearing. The system seamlessly adjusted — the original base layer dissolving as the chosen garments replaced it. The briefs settled against my skin — warm, close, cradling the anatomy between my legs in a soft compression that reduced the ambient sensitivity I'd been carrying since Module 4. The persistent awareness of my vulva against surfaces — the chair pressure, the fabric drag, the open-air vulnerability — muffled to a gentle, bearable hum.

Between the bra and the underwear, I could finally stand without my body screaming at me from six directions simultaneously. The relief was so profound my eyes stung.

<<system "Outerwear selection is aesthetic and functional. The wardrobe provides options calibrated to your avatar's proportions. Choose based on comfort and coverage preference.">>

I reached for the most coverage I could find. A fitted bodysuit in matte black — long sleeves, high neckline, full-length legs. It zipped up the back and sealed around me like a second skin, the smart-fabric adjusting to my contours with a precision that was simultaneously comforting and unsettling. Every curve was visible — the hourglass silhouette was impossible to hide in anything form-fitting — but the fabric was opaque, thick enough to mute environmental sensation, and warm enough to prevent any further temperature-demonstration incidents.

Over that — a cyber-leather jacket, cropped at the waist, with a subtle armored weave through the shoulders and arms. It added structure to my upper body. Made my shoulders feel wider than they were. The collar rose high enough to shield the back of my neck.

I looked in the mirror. A woman looked back — dressed in black, fitted, curved in ways the clothing emphasized rather than concealed. But covered. Protected. Armored. The neon light caught the jacket's seams and threw thin lines of violet across the matte surface. She looked like she belonged in Eidolon. Like she'd been designed for it.

Something in my chest twisted at the thought. I looked away.

<<system "User Avan Morgan. Undergarment fitting.">>

Avan had been dressing on his side of the wardrobe — the privacy screen up between us for the lower-body garments, audio still shared. I'd heard the system's instructions through the frosted light.

<<system "Compression briefs are recommended for daily use. They hold the genitalia against the body, reducing oscillation during movement, protecting the testicles from impact, and minimizing the visibility of involuntary erectile events.">>

<<avan "Minimizing the visibility of—">>

<<system "Erections in this avatar measure 8.4 inches erect. Without compression, tumescence is clearly visible through standard trousers. Compression briefs redirect and contain partial engorgement, reducing visible protrusion by approximately 60%.">>

<<avan "Only 60?">>

<<system "Full erections cannot be entirely concealed by clothing alone. Behavioral management — redirection of attention, repositioning, physical cooling — must supplement garment-based strategies. This will normalize with practice.">>

I heard Avan dressing through the screen. The soft sounds of fabric being pulled on. A sharp intake of breath — the first contact of compression fabric against the glans, probably, the unfamiliar pressure of being *held* rather than hanging free. Then a slow exhale.

<<avan "It's — tight. But better. The — the swinging stops.">>

<<system "Correct. Support eliminates the free-hanging motion that produces discomfort during walking and vulnerability during impact. The compression also reduces friction between the glans and outer clothing, which decreases the frequency of contact-triggered arousal.">>

The privacy screen dropped. Avan stood in front of his wardrobe section, and the transformation was — significant. The compression briefs were invisible beneath dark fitted trousers — tactical cut, reinforced at the thigh, room for the massive quads the system had built him. A fitted undershirt lay flat against his torso, the dark fabric stretched across the terrain of his chest and abdomen without a single wrinkle. Over that — the cyber-leather jacket. Broad-shouldered, armored at the biceps and forearms, the collar standing at his jaw. It made him look like a fortress. A walking, breathing, six-foot-three fortress with forest-green eyes.

He rolled his shoulders. The jacket creaked softly — the leather adjusting, the armored plates settling. His hands emerged from the sleeves — massive, capable, the knuckles visible through the fitted cuffs. He looked down at himself. Turned to the mirror.

The man in the reflection was — striking. Not just large but *composed*. The clothing gave his raw physicality a framework, a context. Instead of a naked collection of overwhelming proportions, he was now a figure. A silhouette. A presence that the eye could process as a *person* rather than a catalogue of intimidating body parts.

But his eyes — Ava's eyes — stared at the reflection with the same dissonance they'd carried since Module 1. Recognition of beauty. No recognition of self.

<<system "Clothing in Eidolon communicates social information. User Leona Hart: your avatar's aesthetic reads as agile, sleek, and guarded. NPCs will interpret dark, covered clothing as a signal of competence and self-possession rather than availability.">>

<<system "User Avan Morgan: your avatar's aesthetic reads as authoritative, capable, and physically dominant. NPCs will interpret armored clothing as a signal of status and territorial confidence. Both aesthetic readings reduce the probability of predatory NPC interaction.">>

<<system "Footwear.">>

Boots materialized at the base of each wardrobe. Mine were low-heeled — a modest three-centimeter lift, enough to shift my posture without destabilizing me. The soles were textured for grip, the ankle support firm. I pulled them on and the extra height barely registered against Avan's towering frame, but the sole's grip on the chrome floor gave me a stability my bare feet had lacked all evening.

Avan's boots were heavy. Magnetic-soled, thick-treaded, built for a body that hit the ground with seismic force at every step. He laced them and the chrome stopped groaning under his footfalls — the boots absorbing impact, dampening the percussion of 188 pounds of muscle meeting polished floor.

<<system "Module 9 complete. Both users are now dressed in avatar-appropriate clothing with functional undergarment support.">>

We stood before the mirror arc — side by side, fully clothed for the first time since the transformation. The reflections showed two people who looked like they belonged in Eidolon City. A woman in black — compact, curved, armored at the shoulders, hair cascading down the jacket's back in honey-brown waves. A man in dark leather — towering, broad, every line of the clothing emphasizing a physicality that filled the frame edge to edge.

We looked like characters. Avatars. The polished, designed residents of a cyberpunk world that dressed its inhabitants in leather and neon and chrome.

We looked nothing like Leo and Ava.

<<leona "We look…">>

<<avan "Yeah.">>

Neither of us finished the thought. The mirrors held us in place — two strangers who were finally dressed, finally covered, finally protected from the raw exposure of the last eight modules — and for the first time since Module 1, the vulnerability wasn't physical. It was something quieter. The small, aching recognition that putting on the armor made the avatars feel more real and the people inside them feel more hidden.

<<system "Module 10 will begin in ninety seconds. Subject: NPC Interactions and Social Dynamics.">>

I tugged the jacket's collar higher. Avan adjusted his cuffs. The city glowed beyond the glass — a world full of NPCs who would see a woman and a man and never know the frightened boy and the trembling girl living inside them.

Ninety seconds.

We were dressed. We were standing. We were still holding hands — leather glove against bare fingers, his grip measured at exactly 3.1 kilograms, the force of someone who had learned what his hands could do and chosen, deliberately, to be gentle.

The walls wove our heartbeats. The boots held us steady. Module 10 was coming, and for the first time, we'd face it armored.

