> Module 12 — REPRODUCTION. The module where the system tells Leo his body can get pregnant and tells Ava her body can cause pregnancy. The most consequential biological reality of the swap laid bare. Contraception options, the real-time pregnancy simulation warning, the testosterone-impaired-judgment caveat. This is where the stakes of their new bodies become permanent-consequence-level real. Let's write it with the full weight it deserves.

[^69]: <output>

:: day_5_25_str

<<system "Module 12: Reproductive Risk and Pregnancy. Initiating.">>

The windows went dark again. The city vanishing behind a new holographic projection — but this time, the display wasn't a timeline or a graph. It was anatomy. Two cross-section diagrams, life-sized, projected side by side across the glass in cool blue light.

On the left: Leona's reproductive system. Ovaries — two small, almond-shaped organs connected to the uterus by fallopian tubes that curved outward like open arms. The uterus itself — pear-shaped, muscular, hollow, its lining rendered in a thin red layer. The cervix at its base. The vaginal canal below, connecting the internal architecture to the world outside.

On the right: Avan's reproductive system. Testes — two dense, oval organs suspended in the scrotum, connected by the vas deferens to the prostate and seminal vesicles. The prostate — a walnut-sized gland positioned below the bladder, its duct feeding into the urethra. The penile shaft in cross-section — the corpora cavernosa flanking the urethra like twin chambers.

Two systems. Designed to interlock. Projected six feet apart on the windows of an apartment occupied by two people who — in their original bodies — had never come close to testing what those systems could do together.

<<system "This module addresses the single highest-consequence capability of your avatars. Reproduction.">>

The word landed in the room like a stone dropped into still water.

<<system "User Leona Hart. Your avatar possesses a fully functional female reproductive system. Ovulation occurs approximately on Day 14 of your hormonal cycle. During the fertile window — Days 10 through 17 — a mature ovum is present in the fallopian tube. If sperm reaches this ovum, fertilization occurs. The fertilized ovum implants in the uterine wall and pregnancy begins.">>

<<system "This is not a simplified simulation. Eidolon's biological modeling is comprehensive. Ovulation, fertilization, implantation, embryonic development, hormonal cascade, and gestational progression are all rendered in real time. Pregnancy in this environment is as real as the body carrying it.">>

I stared at the holographic uterus on the window. At the thin red lining. At the fallopian tubes reaching outward. And the abstract understanding — the intellectual knowledge that had existed somewhere in the background since Module 1 when the system first said *capable of pregnancy* — became concrete. Physical. A room inside my body where a life could begin.

<<leona "How — how pregnant are we talking? Like — simulated symptoms, or—">>

<<system "Full physiological simulation. First trimester: nausea, fatigue, breast tenderness, hormonal intensification. Second trimester: visible abdominal expansion, fetal movement, cardiovascular changes. Third trimester: significant body modification — increased weight, altered center of gravity, reduced mobility, preparation for delivery.">>

<<leona "DELIVERY?">>

<<system "If carried to term. Eidolon's pregnancy simulation runs in real time — approximately 280 days from conception to delivery. The avatar's body changes progressively throughout. These changes are not cosmetic — they alter your physical capabilities, your balance, your stamina, your emotional state, and your appearance. Carrying to term in Eidolon is a significant commitment with irreversible effects on your avatar for the duration.">>

I sat down on the bed edge. Not because the module told me to. Because my legs decided, independently of my brain, that standing was no longer an option while processing the information that my body could grow a person inside it for nine months and then *deliver* it.

<<system "Termination of pregnancy is available through Eidolon's medical district. The procedure requires a district visit, a consultation period, and a recovery interval. It is not instantaneous.">>

<<system "Prevention is preferable to termination. Contraceptive options are available.">>

A compartment in the wardrobe opened — a drawer I hadn't noticed, next to the menstrual products. Inside: a row of small foil packets — barrier contraceptives, Eidolon's equivalent of condoms, sized and shaped for the avatar dimensions the system had catalogued. Beside them: a small vial marked with a medical symbol, connected to the bathroom's delivery panel.

<<system "Barrier methods: physical sheaths that prevent sperm from entering the vaginal canal. Effectiveness is approximately 97% with correct usage. Available in the wardrobe. User Avan Morgan's avatar dimensions have been pre-loaded — the appropriate size is stocked.">>

<<system "Hormonal suppressant: administered via the bathroom medical panel, this protocol suppresses ovulation for a 30-day period, preventing the release of a mature ovum. Effectiveness exceeds 99%. The suppressant may produce mild side effects consistent with hormonal adjustment — breast tenderness, mood fluctuation, minor weight retention.">>

<<system "The system strongly recommends activating at least one contraceptive protocol before any penetrative sexual contact between users. The recommendation is strong — not mandatory. The choice remains with the users.">>

I looked at the foil packets. At the medical vial. At the holographic diagram of my own reproductive system projected across the windows of a cyberpunk apartment. Contraception. For me. Because my body — this body, the one I'd been wearing for three hours — could be made pregnant by the body standing six feet away that belonged to my girlfriend.

The irony was so layered it could have been a doctoral thesis.

<<system "User Avan Morgan.">>

Avan had been standing motionless. Arms at his sides. His face was unreadable — the strong, sculpted features of his avatar locked in the particular stillness of someone processing something too large for expression.

<<system "Your avatar possesses a fully functional male reproductive system. The testes produce sperm continuously. The seminal vesicles and prostate generate ejaculatory fluid. Upon orgasm, the system delivers ejaculate through the urethra.">>

<<system "Ejaculate volume in this avatar is VR-enhanced: 5 to 7 milliliters per release, with higher-than-baseline sperm concentration and motility. In practical terms, this avatar's reproductive output per encounter significantly exceeds average human parameters.">>

Avan's jaw tightened. I watched a muscle flex beneath the bronze skin — the involuntary clench of someone hearing clinical details about what their body could produce.

<<system "If ejaculation occurs inside a fertile female avatar — player or NPC — conception is biologically possible. The probability varies with cycle timing, but during the fertile window, a single unprotected encounter carries a substantial chance of fertilization.">>

<<system "Responsibility for contraception is shared between both users. However, the physical asymmetry is relevant: this avatar's enhanced output increases per-encounter risk. The biological burden of pregnancy falls on the receiving body — in this case, User Leona Hart. But the initiating event — ejaculation — originates from yours.">>

<<avan "So if I — if we — and I don't—">>

<<system "If you ejaculate inside User Leona Hart's vaginal canal during her fertile window without contraceptive measures, pregnancy is a probable outcome. Yes.">>

Avan looked at me. Across the room. Across the six feet of chrome floor and the space between two holographic reproductive systems projected on glass. His green eyes held mine with something I'd never seen in them before — not in Ava's eyes, not in this face. A specific, weighted, gender-inverted recognition.

He was looking at me the way I used to look at Ava. The way a boy looks at a girl when the full, terrifying scope of what their bodies can do *together* lands for the first time. The understanding that desire has a consequence shaped like a human being. And that the consequence lives in *her* body, not his.

Except now it was reversed. The consequence lived in *mine*. And the mechanism lived in his. And both of them knew — from opposite sides of a year-long relationship where this exact fear had never been tested — exactly how heavy that knowledge sat.

<<system "Additional consideration. Withdrawal — removal of the penis before ejaculation — is not a reliable contraceptive method. Pre-ejaculatory fluid, produced involuntarily during arousal, may contain viable sperm cells. Any unprotected penetrative contact carries non-zero conception risk, regardless of whether full ejaculation occurs internally.">>

<<system "Emotional dimension.">>

The display shifted. The anatomical diagrams faded to transparency, and the testosterone waveform from Module 8 reappeared — the jagged cyan spikes, the rapid-onset cascade, the compressed gap between impulse and action.

<<system "Testosterone-driven arousal can impair judgment. During sexual activity, the hormonal cascade intensifies — narrowing focus, accelerating urgency, reducing the cognitive bandwidth available for decision-making. The physical drive toward ejaculation is intense, localized, and progressively harder to interrupt as arousal escalates.">>

<<system "In practical terms: the moment when contraceptive action is most critical — immediately before penetration or ejaculation — is also the moment when the hormonal architecture of this body most strongly resists interruption. The urgency feels real. Overwhelming. The impulse to continue without pause is neurochemically reinforced.">>

<<system "Learning to interrupt this cascade — to pause, to reach for protection, to communicate with your partner — is one of the most important skills for this avatar. It requires practice. It requires conscious override of a powerful biological drive. And it must be established before the moment arrives, not during it.">>

Avan's hands had curled into fists at his sides. Not in anger. In concentration. The deliberate tension of someone committing something to memory with their whole body — not just hearing the warning but *embedding* it, carving it into muscle and bone, because the system had just told him that his body would try to override his brain at the exact moment when his brain's decision mattered most.

<<avan "Before. Not during.">>

<<system "Correct. The decision to use contraception must be made before arousal begins. Once the cascade is active, cognitive resistance diminishes progressively. Planning ahead — ensuring barrier methods are accessible, activating the hormonal suppressant protocol, establishing verbal agreements with your partner — eliminates the need to make critical decisions under hormonal impairment.">>

<<system "Summary.">>

Both diagrams returned to full opacity on the windows. The two systems, side by side, glowing in cool blue light. The tubes reaching. The chambers waiting. The architecture of creation rendered in clinical holographic detail.

<<system "User Leona Hart can become pregnant. User Avan Morgan can cause pregnancy. Both outcomes are real, consequential, and simulated at full biological fidelity. Contraception is available, accessible, and strongly recommended. The choice of if and when to use it belongs to both users.">>

<<system "Module 12 complete.">>

The diagrams faded. Eidolon City returned to the windows — neon rain, hover lanes, the holographic skyline reclaiming the glass. The reproductive systems dissolved into the same light they'd been built from, leaving two people standing in an apartment that knew everything about what their bodies could do to each other.

<<system "Module 13 will begin in ninety seconds. Subject: Arousal, Orgasm, and Sexual Response.">>

Of course. Of course that was next. The system had spent twelve modules building the framework — anatomy, sensation, hormones, consequence — and now it was going to address the act itself. The thing all of it pointed toward. The thing Leo and Ava had spent a year not quite reaching and were now equipped for from opposite directions.

Avan walked to the wardrobe. Reached into the drawer. Picked up one of the foil packets. Turned it over in his massive fingers — the small square looking absurdly tiny in his hand, a fragile barrier against a biological reality the system had just described in milliliters and probability percentages.

He set it on the bedside surface. Didn't open it. Didn't comment. Just placed it there — visible, accessible, present. A decision made before the moment. A plan established outside the cascade.

Then he looked at me. The expression on his face was something I would carry long after this module ended — the quiet, resolved, slightly terrified look of someone who understood the weight of what their body could do and had chosen, consciously, in advance, while the thinking was clear, to be responsible with it.

<<avan "Before. Not during.">>

<<leona "Before. Not during.">>

The foil packet sat on the chrome surface. Small. Shining. The apartment's pulse-veins reflected it in miniature — a tiny square of light repeated in violet and rose across every surface.

Ninety seconds. Module 13.

Neither of us sat. Neither of us spoke. The city rained neon against the glass and the twin heartbeats on the walls pulsed in a rhythm that was accelerating — not with fear, not with dread, but with the specific, charged, anticipatory awareness of two people who knew what was coming next and couldn't decide if they were ready.

