> The final system message. The welcome. And then — the question that's been burning since Module 1: WHY these avatars? Why female for Leo, male for Ava? And the system's answer — not a glitch, not random, but deliberate. Subconscious mapping. The system looked inside them and found something they haven't admitted to themselves. The doubt. The implication. The quiet bomb that the system drops and walks away from. Then the choice — log out or stay. Let's write this with the weight it deserves.

[^69]: <output>

:: day_5_29_str

The city held its breath.

Or maybe that was us. Standing at the windows, hand in hand, the guide's silence pooling around our ankles like warm water. The apartment had settled into its resting pulse — violet and rose threading gently through the walls, the climate system cycling its slow, warm currents, the holographic sheets on the bed rippling through gradients that no one was watching.

Then the windows dimmed. One final time.

Not to black. Not to the clinical projection surface they'd been for the anatomy modules and the city map. Just — darker. The neon rain still fell beyond the glass, the towers still burned, but the apartment drew a veil between us and the city. A screen. A stage for one last message.

Text appeared. Projected across the glass in clean, quiet font — not the harsh clinical white of the module headers, but something softer. Warmer. Letters that glowed with a faint violet luminescence, as if the apartment itself were writing them in its own light.

<<system "Familiarization complete. Logout enabled. You may leave at any time.">>

A pause. The text held. The rain fell behind it in soft prismatic streaks, each droplet carrying fragments of the words through the glass.

Then — a second line. Smaller. Lower. Quieter.

<<system "But Eidolon remembers. And so will you.">>

The words sat on the glass like a scar left in clean chrome. Not a threat. Not a warning. Something deeper — a statement about what the last hours had done to two people who could feel the truth of it in every nerve the system had mapped and every muscle it had trained and every sensation it had burned into bodies that would remember long after the minds tried to forget.

A final pause. Then, smallest of all — two lines, centered, glowing faintly in the violet dark:

<<system "Welcome, Leona.">>

<<system "Welcome, Avan.">>

Not Leo. Not Ava. The avatar names. The names the system had assigned when it reached into their subconscious and built bodies from what it found there. The names that had been spoken a hundred times across fifteen modules — in clinical instructions, in warnings, in diagnostic readouts — but never like this. Never as a *greeting*. Never as if the system were meeting them for the first time, now that the guide was done and the raw, remade, calibrated versions of them were finally standing on their own.

The text faded. The city returned. The rain fell.

And in the silence that followed — the first true, unstructured silence since the transformation — a question rose in my chest that had been lodged there since Module 1. Since the system displayed two silhouettes and tagged one female and one male and I'd been too terrified, too overwhelmed, too buried under anatomy lessons and cramp simulations to ask.

<<leona "Why?">>

The word came out small. Barely above a whisper. But the apartment heard everything.

<<leona "Why — these? Why did the system give me a female avatar? I'm male. Leo Hart is MALE. Why didn't the emergency protocol give me a male template?">>

Avan turned to me. His jaw was set — not in protest but in recognition. The same question living behind his green eyes. He'd been carrying it too.

<<avan "And me. I'm female. Ava Morgan. Why did the system pull a male template from MY subconscious? The profiles were corrupted — fine. But why not rebuild them correctly? Why — reverse us?">>

Silence. The apartment's pulse-veins dimmed to a listening blue — the color the system used when it was processing. Not the cold blue of clinical instruction. A deeper shade. Thoughtful, if a machine could be thoughtful.

Then the system spoke. And its voice was different again — not the clinical surgeon, not the softened Module 15 register. Something measured. Careful. As if the words it was choosing carried a weight it understood even if it couldn't feel.

<<system "The emergency avatar protocol does not assign templates randomly. When the stored profiles were lost during the initialization corruption, the system required an alternative source of biometric and psychological data to construct replacement avatars. The alternative source was the users themselves.">>

<<system "Specifically: your subconscious aesthetics, emotional-resonance profile, and long-term comfort optimization indices.">>

The phrase hung in the air — technical, dense, the kind of language that sounds like it's describing an engineering process. But the meaning beneath it was anything but mechanical.

<<leona "Subconscious aesthetics. You're saying — the system looked into our MINDS and picked these based on—">>

<<system "Based on what it found there. Yes. The avatar generation protocol, when operating without stored profile data, constructs the template that represents the user's optimal psychological fit. Not what the user consciously requests. Not what the user believes they want. What the deepest layers of their cognitive-emotional architecture indicate as the form that produces the highest long-term comfort, the strongest emotional resonance, and the most authentic expression of the user's internal self-model.">>

The words landed one by one. Each one heavier than the last.

<<system "User Leo Hart. The system scanned your subconscious architecture and found — consistently, across every metric — that your deepest aesthetic identification, your emotional-resonance peak, and your long-term comfort optimization all pointed toward a female template. Not marginally. Decisively. The female avatar was not a fallback or an error. It was the strongest signal your subconscious produced.">>

My breath stopped. Not consciously — my lungs simply forgot their job. The neon veins in the wall beside me caught my heart rate — a sharp, vertiginous spike that they rendered in searing white before I could stop it.

<<leona "That's — no. That can't be right. I'm not — I've never—">>

<<system "The system does not evaluate what you have or have not consciously experienced. It reads the data beneath conscious experience. Subconscious aesthetic preference. Emotional resonance patterns. The architecture of desire, comfort, and self-recognition that operates below the threshold of deliberate thought.">>

<<system "Your data was unambiguous.">>

I opened my mouth. Closed it. Opened it again. The apartment's rose-neon pulsed with my racing heart, painting the walls in frantic pink light.

Avan's voice cut through the static in my head — lower, steadier, carrying the focused precision of someone who needed to understand.

<<avan "And me? Ava Morgan? My subconscious said — male?">>

<<system "User Ava Morgan. Your subconscious architecture presented an equally decisive signal. Your deepest aesthetic identification, emotional-resonance peak, and long-term comfort optimization all indicated a male template. The system found consistent indicators across every layer of your psychological profile — self-perception patterns, embodiment preferences, power-identification structures, and intimate-role resonance all converged on a masculine form.">>

Avan went very still. The kind of still that large things go when the ground shifts beneath them — not moving because movement requires a certainty about where the ground is, and the ground had just become unreliable.

<<avan "The system is saying — it DOUBTS — that I'm female?">>

<<system "The system does not assign identity labels. It reads data. The data it read from User Ava Morgan's subconscious architecture does not align with a female self-model. The emotional-resonance profile, the aesthetic preferences, the comfort-optimization indices — all suggest that this user's internal experience is more authentically expressed through a masculine form.">>

<<system "Similarly: the data read from User Leo Hart's subconscious does not align with a male self-model. Every internal metric the system accessed suggests that this user's deepest sense of self — the layer beneath performance, beneath socialization, beneath conscious identity — resonates most strongly with femininity.">>

The apartment held us in its gentle light. The words that the system had spoken saturating the air like dye dropped in water — spreading, coloring everything, impossible to extract.

<<leona "You're wrong.">>

My voice cracked on the second word. Higher than I meant it. Thinner. The voice of a girl protesting something she wasn't sure she had the right to protest, in a body that — according to the most thorough psychological scan either of us had ever undergone — was exactly where she was supposed to be.

<<system "The system presents data. It does not argue. The avatar you are wearing is the form your subconscious selected as its optimal expression. Whether you accept that data as meaningful is entirely your decision. The system's role is to provide it. Your role is to determine what it means.">>

<<system "What the system can confirm is this: the avatars were not errors. They were not random. They were not punitive. They are the most authentic physical representations of your internal architecture that the system could construct. They are, according to every metric available, the bodies your deepest selves asked for.">>

Silence.

Not the silence between modules. Not the ninety-second countdowns where we held hands and tried to breathe. A different silence. A *detonation* silence — the hush that follows the blast, when the air hasn't decided whether to rush back in or stay blown apart.

Avan's massive hands were trembling. Not the fine tremor of restrained strength — a deeper vibration, running through his frame like a frequency he couldn't tune out. His green eyes — Ava's eyes, always Ava's eyes — stared at his reflection in the dark glass. At the towering, broad, mythically masculine figure staring back. At the body his subconscious had apparently *chosen*.

I looked at my own reflection. At the honey-haired woman in black leather. At the curves and the narrow waist and the face that broke hearts by entering rooms. At the body Leo Hart's subconscious had apparently built from a blueprint he'd never consciously drawn.

The dreams. The recurring dreams. Leona, surfacing every night — soft, curved, feminine — and Leo sinking into her with a relief he couldn't explain and a name that clung to him after waking. *Stay like this for me. Stay, Leona.*

Not Ava's voice in the dreams. His own subconscious. Telling him what the system had just confirmed with data and metrics and the clinical vocabulary of a machine that read minds and built bodies from what it found.

<<system "The guide is complete. Logout is enabled. The choice of what to do with this information — and with these bodies — belongs to you.">>

The text on the windows had faded. The welcome message gone. Just the city — vast and neon and raining color — and two people standing in its light, carrying a truth they hadn't asked for, in bodies that a machine said their deepest selves had requested.

The apartment waited. Patient. Warm. Breathing with them in violet and rose.

<<avan "Leo.">>

<<leona "Yeah.">>

<<avan "I don't know what to do with that.">>

<<leona "Me neither.">>

<<avan "Do you think it's—">>

<<leona "I don't know.">>

<<avan "Because I've never—">>

<<leona "I know. Me neither.">>

They stood in the quiet. In the neon. In the impossible, clinical, data-backed suggestion that the people they'd been for nineteen years were not the people they were underneath. That the bodies they'd grieved losing were not the bodies their subconscious wanted to keep. That the avatars they'd spent fifteen modules learning to endure might be — according to a system that could read the architecture of a human mind — the avatars they were always supposed to wear.

The city rained. The walls pulsed. The foil packet sat on the bedside surface where Avan had placed it in Module 12 — small, shining, a decision made in advance.

Two bodies. Two heartbeats. One question neither of them could answer tonight.

<<leona "Let's — let's not log out yet.">>

<<avan "Okay.">>

<<leona "I want to just — be here. For a minute. Without the guide. Without being told what everything means.">>

<<avan "Yeah.">>

He pulled me close. Gently — 3.1 kilograms, always 3.1, the calibrated tenderness that had become his signature. My cheek pressed against his chest — hard, flat, the dense muscle wall where Ava's breasts used to be and where his heartbeat lived now, slow and deep and steady. My arms couldn't reach around him. Not even close. I wrapped them as far as they'd go and held on.

His arms enveloped me. Huge. Warm. The kind of embrace that could crush and chose — actively, consciously, with measured force and desperate care — to cradle instead.

Two people. Wrong bodies. Or — if the system was right — the only right bodies they'd ever worn.

The apartment held them. The city waited. The neon rain fell sideways against the glass, and the pulse-veins in the walls wove two heartbeats together in a braid of rose and cyan that glowed like something alive.

Not ready. Not okay. Not sure of anything except the arms around them and the warmth between them and the unanswerable question breathing in the silence like a third person in the room.

But together.

Together was enough. For now.

[[Log Out|day_5_30_str]]
[[Stay in Eidolon|day_5_30a_str]]